        "eventMetadata": {
          "context_description": "Recent U.S. inflation readings have moderated modestly, with July CPI at 3.4% year-over-year and core at 2.5%, yet remaining well above the Federal Reserve’s 2% target after years of elevated pressures tied partly to energy costs. The July employment report surprised to the downside with a 23,000 payroll decline and downward revisions, pushing the unemployment rate to 4.1% and tempering expectations for immediate tightening. At its July meeting, the FOMC voted 9-3 to hold the federal funds rate at 3.50%-3.75%, though three regional presidents dissented in favor of a hike amid persistent price concerns. Fed Governor Waller’s September 3 remarks highlighted ongoing disinflation signals while leaving room for a September 15-16 move if August data prove hotter. These crosscurrents—sticky inflation versus softening labor conditions—underpin the tight split between no change and a 25-basis-point increase in trader pricing.",
